BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:History Talks - Three Pioneers
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T113000
UID:260623WNCJGGNmwUC7DK2017kFiA
DESCRIPTION:Carrillo-Fitch\, Heald\, Matheson\n\nClaire O’Neill\, Nita M
 iller\, Don Anderson\n\nLearn about three Healdsburg pioneers\, all of who
 m shaped Healdsburg’s history in of the Gold Rush. Claire O’Neill will
  introduce you to Josefa Carrillo Fitch\, from a San Diego Spanish family\
 , who arrived after her husband’s death and discovered that squatters we
 re inhabiting much of the land her family owned as the Rancho Sotoyome lan
 d grant.\n\nNita Miller will talk about Harmon Heald\, one of those squatt
 ers and a failed gold seeker from Ohio. He eventually created a subdivisio
 n on land he purchased from the Fitch family and that became the center of
  the town named for him.\n\nAnd Don Anderson will present the life of Rode
 rick Matheson\, a Scotsman\, who found his way to Healdsburg where he was 
 a civic leader\, and then met his death during the Civil War after accepti
 ng a commission from Lincoln.
LOCATION:Healdsburg Plaza Gazebo
URL:https://www.healdsburgmuseum.org/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SORBET Pre-Harvest Dinner at Rossi Ranch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T180000
UID:260622JWR1JDZGkE6bsDsMQNx2TQ
DESCRIPTION:We're delighted to celebrate the height of the growing season
  with SORBET's Summer Dinner at Rossi Ranch. Join us for an evening of con
 nection\, conversation\, and community\, with the opportunity to spend tim
 e one-on-one with local growers and producers who are shaping the future o
 f Sonoma Valley agriculture. \n\nEnjoy a thoughtfully prepared meal featu
 ring ingredients sourced from farmers committed to the health of our land\
 , our communities\, and our planet. Food will be provided and prepared by 
 Allikai Eatery’s own Chef Fiorella.Set against the backdrop of Rossi Ran
 ch's historic vineyards and organic farming practices\, the evening offers
  a chance to gather just as the precipice of the 2026 harvest season. \n\
 nTicket sales and donations will support SORBET's ongoing mission to adva
 nce  and educate on organic\, regenerative and biodynamic winegrowin
 g in Sonoma Valley. Guests will also have the opportunity to participate
  in a raffle featuring a selection of special bottles generously donated b
 y local producers. \n\nAdditional Information: 21+. Sandals and heels are
  not recommended. 10 – 15 SORBET winemakers will be present. Food and w
 ine are included with ticket price.
LOCATION:Rossi Ranch
URL:https://www.zeffy.com/en-US/ticketing/sorbet-pre-harvest-dinner-at-ros
 si-ranch
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:“The Water Baby” read live at Jack London State Historic Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T190000
UID:2606224Rmc-3t81EGB-JGvowLwLA
DESCRIPTION:Storyteller Jeff Falconer will present a live reading of Jack 
 London’s short story “The Water Baby” in the historic cottage garden
 .\n\nPublished in London’s Hawaiian story collection “*On the Makaloa 
 Mat*\,” this is a reflective tale that explores childhood\, imagination\
 , and humanity’s connection to the natural world. Set against a backdrop
  of water and wonder\, the story offers a glimpse into a lesser-known side
  of London’s writing.\n\nGuests are invited to gather beside the garden 
 pond and experience the story as it was meant to be shared—through the s
 poken word. Following the reading\, participants will have an opportunity 
 to discuss the story’s themes and place within Jack London’s body of w
 ork.\n\nThis intimate program is part of the year-long celebration of Jack
  London’s 150th birthday and offers a unique chance to engage with one o
 f his lesser-known short stories.\n\n**Interested in reading along? Copies
  of “*On the Makaloa Mat*\,” the collection containing Jack London’s
  short story “The Water Baby\,” are available for purchase. Visit the 
 park’s Museum Bookstore or order a copy at https://jacklondonpark.com/pr
 oduct/on-the-makaloa-mat/.**
LOCATION:Jack London State Historic Park
URL:https://jacklondonpark.com/events/read-aloud-the-water-baby/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
